Selenium Interview Questions & Answers
1. What is Selenium WebDriver?

Selenium WebDriver is an open-source tool used to automate web browsers. It helps us perform actions like clicking buttons, entering text, and testing web applications automatically.

2. Why did you use Maven with Selenium?

Maven is used to manage project dependencies, build the project, and execute test cases, whereas Selenium is used for browser automation.

3. What is Page Object Model (POM)?

Page Object Model is a design pattern in which we create separate classes for different web pages. It improves code reusability, readability, and maintainability.

4. Where did you use inheritance in your project?

I used inheritance by extending BaseTest in my test classes. It allows them to reuse common browser setup and teardown methods without writing the same code repeatedly.

5. What is the difference between findElement() and findElements()?
- findElement() returns the first matching web element and throws an exception if no element is found.
- findElements() returns a list of all matching elements and returns an empty list if none are found.
6. What do @BeforeEach and @AfterEach do?

@BeforeEach runs before every test case and is used to initialize the browser. @AfterEach runs after every test case and is used to close the browser.

7. What is Parameterized Testing?

Parameterized testing allows us to execute the same test case multiple times with different sets of input data. I used JUnit 5's @ParameterizedTest and @CsvSource for testing different login credentials.

8. What is the difference between Explicit Wait and Implicit Wait?
- Implicit Wait: Applies a common waiting time while searching for elements throughout the driver session.
- Explicit Wait: Waits for a specific condition, such as an element becoming clickable or visible.
9. Why does StaleElementReferenceException occur?

It occurs when an element that was previously located is no longer attached to the current DOM. This can happen when a page refreshes or its elements are updated.

10. What is the purpose of Assertions?

Assertions are used to verify whether the actual result matches the expected result. They help determine whether a test case has passed or failed.